by nicolas-grekas at 2026-02-18T21:13:37Z

The serializer can't route failures through the bus on its own: it has no access to that infrastructure. What it can do is throw, which is what it already does. The transport is the natural catch point since it owns the `decode()` call and the raw payload. The result (`FailedEnvelopeDecodingMessage`) is a thin value object with no transport knowledge; the actual re-decode logic lives in the middleware, which does have bus context. So responsibility is actually well separated: transport catches + wraps, middleware re-decodes + continues.

The only way to move this concern really self-contained in the serializer would be to make it return an `Envelope<FailedEnvelopeDecodingMessage>` on failure instead of throwing. That'b be a BC break to `SerializerInterface`, and that'd force all custom serializers to know about `FailedEnvelopeDecodingMessage`. It'd conflate "I decoded this" with "I failed to decode this" in the same return type. That'd be worse separation IMHO, not better.

